```apex
trigger OpportunityTrigger on Opportunity (before insert, before update, before delete,
    after insert, after update, after delete, after undelete) {

    fflib_SObjectDomain.triggerHandler(Opportunities.class);
}
```

Notes on the implementation:

- The trigger covers all seven events and delegates entirely to `fflib_SObjectDomain.triggerHandler(Opportunities.class)`.
- `getNewlyWonIds()` builds the qualifying set per invocation: on insert, records with `StageName = 'Closed Won'` qualify; on update, only those whose prior stage (from `getOriginalRecords()`) was *not* `Closed Won` — so re-editing an already-won opportunity never qualifies again.
- `startFulfilment` is called exactly once per trigger invocation (the domain class is instantiated once per event per invocation, and we aggregate ids into a single set), and never when the set is empty.
- The service is resolved through `Application.Service.newInstance(...)`, so `Application.Service.setMock` in tests is honored; `inherited sharing` keeps sharing behavior consistent with the caller.

