```apex
public inherited sharing class AccountOnboardingService {

    public static List<Account> createAccountsWithContacts(Map<String, List<String>> contactLastNamesByAccountName) {
        List<Account> newAccounts = new List<Account>();

        if (contactLastNamesByAccountName == null || contactLastNamesByAccountName.isEmpty()) {
            return newAccounts;
        }

        // Order matters: Parents (Account) before Children (Contact)
        fflib_SObjectUnitOfWork uow = new fflib_SObjectUnitOfWork(
            new List<SObjectType>{ Account.SObjectType, Contact.SObjectType }
        );

        for (String accountName : contactLastNamesByAccountName.keySet()) {
            Account acct = new Account(Name = accountName);
            uow.registerNew(acct);
            newAccounts.add(acct);

            List<String> lastNames = contactLastNamesByAccountName.get(accountName);
            if (lastNames == null) {
                continue;
            }

            for (String lastName : lastNames) {
                if (String.isBlank(lastName)) {
                    continue;
                }
                uow.registerNew(new Contact(LastName = lastName), Contact.AccountId, acct);
            }
        }

        if (!newAccounts.isEmpty()) {
            uow.commitWork();
        }

        return newAccounts;
    }
}
```

Notes on the implementation:

- A single `fflib_SObjectUnitOfWork` is created per call, with the sObject types registered in parent-first order (`Account`, then `Contact`), so accounts are inserted before contacts in one logical unit.
- Child records are registered via `uow.registerNew(record, Contact.AccountId, parent)`, which lets the UoW automatically stamp the parent’s Id onto each contact after the account insert — no manual DML anywhere.
- Null/empty map (or an account name mapping to a null/empty list) results in no DML and an empty (or Id-less accounts only) result; blank last names are skipped defensively.
- Bulk safety: all records are registered in memory and committed by `commitWork()` in one go, with no DML or SOQL inside any loop.
- The class is `inherited sharing` so it respects the sharing context of its caller, following Apex security best practice for service-layer classes.

