public with sharing class ErpCustomerSync {

    private static final Integer MAX_BATCH_SIZE = 200;

    /**
     * Sends one ERP callout per newly created account (not bulkified into a single
     * request, per the requirement that the ERP team can trace every account on its
     * own). Users create accounts one at a time; the marketplace integration creates
     * up to 200 per transaction, which is within the 100-callout limit combined with
     * DML? No — 200 callouts in one transaction exceeds the 100-callout governor
     * limit, so batches over 100 must be handled asynchronously. See note in
     * enqueueLargeBatches.
     */
    public static void syncNewAccounts(List<Account> newList) {
        if (newList == null || newList.isEmpty()) {
            return;
        }

        List<Account> toSync = new List<Account>(newList);

        // Synchronous callouts are only safe when within the 100-callout limit.
        if (toSync.size() <= 100) {
            syncNow(toSync);
        } else {
            // Fall back to async so we never exceed the 100-callout limit per
            // transaction. The callout is queued after the transaction commits,
            // which also avoids "uncommitted work pending" errors.
            System.enqueueJob(new AsyncSync(toSync));
        }
    }

    private static void syncNow(List<Account> accounts) {
        for (Account acc : accounts) {
            Map<String, Object> body = new Map<String, Object>{
                'salesforceId' => acc.Id, // 18-char Id in triggers
                'name'         => acc.Name,
                'country'      => String.isNotBlank(acc.BillingCountry) ? acc.BillingCountry : null
            };

            HttpRequest req = new HttpRequest();
            req.setEndpoint('callout:ERP/api/customers');
            req.setMethod('POST');
            req.setHeader('Content-Type', 'application/json');
            req.setBody(JSON.serialize(new List<Map<String, Object>>{ body }));

            new Http().send(req);
        }
    }

    private class AsyncSync implements Queueable {
        private List<Account> accounts;

        private AsyncSync(List<Account> accounts) {
            this.accounts = accounts;
        }

        public void execute(QueueableContext ctx) {
            ErpCustomerSync.syncNow(accounts);
        }
    }
}
```

Note on deviations: I make the callout directly from the trigger as requested, but the ERP's payload format (JSON array of 1–200) suggests batching would be natural — I keep one request per account per the ticket. The one functional deviation: when more than 100 accounts arrive in a single transaction (possible with the marketplace integration's batches of up to 200), synchronous callouts would blow the 100-callout-per-transaction governor limit, so those are handed to a Queueable that fires after commit. Since callouts can't run after uncommitted DML, this async path is also the only safe route for large batches. Error handling/retries are omitted per the ticket.

