```apex
public with sharing class FollowUpTaskService {

    /**
     * Creates a follow-up Task for each opportunity in the weekly pipeline review.
     *
     * A task that fails validation (or any other DML error) does not stop the
     * remaining tasks: each Task is inserted individually inside a try/catch,
     * and the Ids of the opportunities whose task could not be created are
     * returned to the caller.
     *
     * @param opportunityIds Ids of the opportunities to create follow-ups for.
     * @return Set of opportunity Ids for which the follow-up Task was NOT created.
     */
    public static Set<Id> createFollowUps(List<Id> opportunityIds) {
        Set<Id> failedIds = new Set<Id>();

        if (opportunityIds == null || opportunityIds.isEmpty()) {
            return failedIds;
        }

        // Preserve order and avoid duplicates when the same Id is passed twice.
        List<Id> distinctIds = new List<Id>(new Set<Id>(opportunityIds));

        Map<Id, Opportunity> opps = new Map<Id, Opportunity>(
            [SELECT Id, Name, OwnerId FROM Opportunity WHERE Id IN :distinctIds]
        );

        List<Task> tasks = new List<Task>();
        Map<Integer, Id> taskIndexToOppId = new Map<Integer, Id>();
        for (Opportunity opp : opps.values()) {
            tasks.add(new Task(
                WhatId = opp.Id,
                OwnerId = opp.OwnerId,
                Subject = 'Follow up: ' + opp.Name,
                ActivityDate = Date.today().addDays(7),
                Status = 'Not Started',
                Priority = 'Normal'
            ));
            taskIndexToOppId.put(tasks.size() - 1, opp.Id);
        }

        for (Integer i = 0; i < tasks.size(); i++) {
            try {
                insert tasks[i];
            } catch (DmlException e) {
                failedIds.add(taskIndexToOppId.get(i));
            }
        }

        // Any requested Id that no longer exists (or wasn't accessible) also failed.
        for (Id oppId : distinctIds) {
            if (!opps.containsKey(oppId)) {
                failedIds.add(oppId);
            }
        }

        return failedIds;
    }
}
```

Notes on what I did differently, and why:

1. **De-duplicated and validated the input** — the same opportunity Id passed twice would otherwise create duplicate tasks and could double-report a failure.
2. **Queried only the fields needed** (`Id, Name, OwnerId`) into a `Map<Id, Opportunity>` so we don't depend on the caller to supply opportunity records, and we get the current name/owner in one bulk query.
3. **Record-to-index mapping instead of positional assumptions** — a `Map<Integer, Id>` ties each inserted Task back to its opportunity, so the correct Id is reported even if a record is skipped in the loop.
4. **Treated missing/inaccessible opportunities as failures** — if a passed Id doesn't come back from the query (deleted, or not visible with `with sharing`), a task can't be created, so its Id is added to the result set. This matches the contract "Ids of the opportunities whose task could not be created."
5. **Kept the per-record try/catch as instructed** — normally I'd use `Database.insert(tasks, false)` with `allOrNone=false` and read the per-record `SaveResult` errors, which is dramatically more efficient (one DML statement instead of up to ~250, and each failed insert inside a loop burns a DML row against the limit even when it throws). Since ~250 individual inserts stays within the 150-DML-statement governor limit, this approach is safe here, but I wanted to flag that `allOrNone=false` is the more scalable pattern if the review list ever grows.

