```apex
public with sharing class AccountCountrySync {

    public static void syncContactsOnCountryChange(
        List<Account> newAccounts,
        Map<Id, Account> oldAccountMap
    ) {
        // 1. Find only the accounts whose BillingCountry actually changed.
        List<Id> changedAccountIds = new List<Id>();
        for (Account acc : newAccounts) {
            Account oldAcc = oldAccountMap.get(acc.Id);
            String newCountry = acc.BillingCountry;
            String oldCountry = oldAcc != null ? oldAcc.BillingCountry : null;
            if (newCountry != oldCountry) {
                changedAccountIds.add(acc.Id);
            }
        }
        if (changedAccountIds.isEmpty()) {
            return;
        }

        // 2. Load only the contacts of those accounts, grouped by AccountId.
        Map<Id, List<Contact>> contactsByAccount = new Map<Id, List<Contact>>();
        for (Contact con : [
            SELECT Id, AccountId, MailingCountry
            FROM Contact
            WHERE AccountId IN :changedAccountIds
        ]) {
            if (!contactsByAccount.containsKey(con.AccountId)) {
                contactsByAccount.put(con.AccountId, new List<Contact>());
            }
            contactsByAccount.get(con.AccountId).add(con);
        }

        // 3. Update only contacts whose MailingCountry is blank or matches the old country.
        Map<Id, Account> newAccountMap = new Map<Id, Account>(newAccounts);
        List<Contact> toUpdate = new List<Contact>();
        for (Id accId : changedAccountIds) {
            Account oldAcc = oldAccountMap.get(accId);
            String oldCountry = oldAcc.BillingCountry;
            String newCountry = newAccountMap.get(accId).BillingCountry;

            for (Contact con : contactsByAccount.get(accId)) {
                String mc = con.MailingCountry;
                if (mc == null || mc.trim().length() == 0 || mc == oldCountry) {
                    con.MailingCountry = newCountry;
                    toUpdate.add(con);
                }
            }
        }

        if (!toUpdate.isEmpty()) {
            update toUpdate;
        }
    }
}
```

**What I changed and why:**

1. **I did not run `SELECT Id, AccountId, MailingCountry FROM Contact` unfiltered.** With ~2.3 million contacts that query will either blow past the 50,000-row Apex query limit immediately, or (if it somehow ran) return 2.3M rows into heap memory and crash. The trigger would fail on the very first MDM batch. Instead I query once, filtered by `AccountId IN :changedAccountIds` — still exactly one query per trigger invocation (bulk-safe, no queries in loops), but it returns only the contacts of the ~200 accounts actually in the batch. The "load everything once and pick from the map" goal is preserved; the map is just scoped to the data you need.

2. **Comparison details:** I compare `BillingCountry` with `!=` on Strings, which treats `null` and `''` as unequal in Apex — so clearing a country or setting it from blank still counts as a change. I also trim when checking for a blank `MailingCountry` so whitespace-only values are treated as blank.

3. **I only `update` the contacts that actually change**, leaving "works abroad" contacts and unchanged-account contacts untouched, as required. Skipped contacts never enter the DML list, so they also don't count against DML row limits.

4. **Guard clauses** (`isEmpty()` checks) keep the handler cheap when nothing changed, avoiding an unnecessary query and DML statement.

